Original commit body from d37a307d9df552474dd912f58b08c0381c941e40:

Per ADR-0036 (D1a) the path slot is the one colon-TOLERANT slot in
{lang}:{path}:{span}:{name}:{kind}, so the parse must be right-anchored.
That fact had four homes and two took parts[1]:

  ir._extract_path_slot            parts[1]         WRONG
  ir._parse_dangling_id            right-anchored   correct
  verify_claims._symbol_path_slot  right-anchored   correct
  taint._extract_callee_module     parts[1]         WRONG

The two in ir.py sit thirteen lines apart. _symbol_path_slot's docstring had
already named _extract_path_slot as wrong and warned "this must not become a
third naive copy" -- while a fourth copy was live in taint.py. All four now
delegate to ir.symbol_path_slot.

WHY THIS IS A DEFECT AND NOT TIDINESS. Rust is the only language whose taint
catalogue uses :: module paths, and all nine of its distinct sink modules are
colon-bearing (std::fs, std::process, std::io, std::net::TcpStream, ...).
parts[1] truncated every one to 'std'; _module_matches('std::fs','std') is
False; and _lookup_named_entry treats a present-but-mismatched module as a
REJECTION rather than a degrade. The outcome was inverted -- the edge that
correctly named its module lost to the edge that named none:

  rust:std::fs:0-0:write:external_symbol    hits=3 -> MATCH=None
  rust:external:0-0:write:external_symbol   hits=3 -> MATCH=std::fs.write

_extract_callee_module's docstring was the tell: it claimed to mirror
_extract_callee_name's parsing, which IS the right-anchored scan, while the
body did another one (L50 -- a comment asserting a false fact supplies a
rationale, and a rationale forecloses the fix).

MEASURED, on a 9-repo cohort (95,950 taint call edges, population selected by
production's own _is_taint_call_edge): +1 gained, 0 lost, 0 re-targeted,
recovering rust:std::env:0-0:var_os -> std::env.var_os, a real
environment-variable source. Predicted by simulation before the edit and
reproduced exactly by the shipped code. The non-destructiveness clause was
asserted first (L57): the correct result here is that nothing is lost, and an
implementation that dropped matches would have looked like a precision win.

The effect is small today only because Rust analyzers emit the 'external'
sentinel for 396 of 449 unresolved taint call edges, bypassing the module
filter, while the 53 carrying real module paths name non-catalogued callees --
so their rejections were accidentally correct. When external-id module coverage
rises (WI-jokij), those 396 start carrying std::fs, and an unfixed truncation
would take Rust from 45 matched sinks to zero. WI-jokij is blocked on this.

Refs: WI-lonad (re-scoped), WI-jokij, INV-karud (a1)
